WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.253
- Hi, good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.230
Welcome.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.640
My name is Priya Natarajan,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.230
I'm the current director

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:17.370
of The Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities at Yale,

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.100
our flagship interdisciplinary program.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.250
I am delighted to invite you all back

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.820
to today's discussion session following

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.800
a fantastic Colloquium yesterday,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.760
given by Professor Brian Cantwell Smith.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.050
The title of his colloquium

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.900
was "Inference in a Non-Conceptual World."

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.560
And as part of the Nature
of Inference Series,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.100
it has been our practice
to follow-up the Colloquium

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.210
by a distinguished
speaker with a discussion

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.240
and with a conversant,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.060
a distinguished researcher,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.420
who might have a slightly
different perspective

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.330
from the Colloquium
speaker the previous day.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.510
And it is my absolute honor today

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.010
to invite Professor Joseph Rouse

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.990
from Wesleyan University

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.023
to be the conversant and
lead discussant today.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.040
Professor Rouse's primary
research interests

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.050
are in the philosophy of science,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.090
and the history of 20th
century philosophy,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.160
interdisciplinary science studies,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.410
and the social theory of practices.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.290
Within these areas, his
primary foci include

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.240
the philosophy of scientific practice,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.050
naturalism and anti-naturalism
in 20th century philosophy,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.730
the metaphysics of normativity,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.320
the connections between the analytic

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.180
and continental philosophy,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.770
the relation of philosophy of science

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.570
to philosophy of mind and language,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.530
and to metaphysics,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.150
cultural studies of science

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.070
and feminist science studies.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.110
He has many,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.220
he's authored many books

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.807
and he has a current
manuscript under review,

00:01:52.807 --> 00:01:56.340
"Social Practices as
Biological Niche Construction"

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.160
and in which he shows how and why

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.710
to integrate our biological conception

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.120
of human beings as animals

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.120
and our social cultural and
psychological conceptions

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.950
of human beings as persons
and acculturate agents.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.370
So it is a real,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:19.370
it's gonna be a real treat today
to witness this discussion,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.650
interaction, and conversation.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.150
And as per custom,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.050
I'd like to invite you
all to post your questions

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.710
in the Q and A feature of the webinar.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.850
And before I turn over the
floor to Professor Rouse,

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:36.870
I would just like to,

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.510
once again thank the support

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.530
from Mr. and Mrs. Richard
and Barbara Franke,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.300
as well as the Templeton Foundation

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.260
that have made possible
this Inference Series.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.860
And I also want to remind you
that we are recording this,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.350
and therefore, all
participants are required

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.260
to mute their audio and videos.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.980
So welcome once again, everyone.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.030
And, Professor Rouse, the floor is yours.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.863
Thank you so much to both of you

00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:09.850
and, Brian, for you to come back again

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.630
to participate in this discussion.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.650
- Okay, well, let me
begin by sharing my screen

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.660
and then by first thanking Ty Kamp

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.630
and Priya Natarajan for the invitation,

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.970
The Franke Center for the sponsorship,

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.830
and especially, Brian Cantwell Smith,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.353
for his very rich and provocative talk.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.830
Has taken on the fraught domain

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.950
of the relation between
thought and or language

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.630
and perception in action.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.840
And with it, the demarcation
of conceptual content

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.600
from whatever is Non-Conceptual.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.330
As he notes in the case
of Evanson McDowell,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.180
disagreements in this domain often turn

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.400
on differing intuitions about,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.600
or conceptions of what is
conceptual or non-conceptual

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.720
with proponents then
talking through one another.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.400
And one constant difficulty

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.970
is to secure non-question
begging characterizations

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:04.443
of what we're talking about.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.860
Brian and I also differ in some respects

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.350
in the end about what
is or is not conceptual,

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:14.013
but I'm confident we can avoid
such unproductive failures.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.680
So, I'm gonna quickly overview

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.594
the themes of the talk.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:25.140
And first, I'm gonna talk
about four central aspects

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.650
of Brian's talk, with which
I'm largely in agreement.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.680
First, what he calls an ontological,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.580
rather than an epistemic
understanding of the conceptual

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.680
&amp; "non-conceptual" content distinction.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.593
Second, his emphasis on
the fine-grained richness

00:04:41.593 --> 00:04:44.160
&amp; continuity of the world,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.400
within which conceptual
understanding is situated

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.740
and to which it is accountable.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.580
Third, we agree on many
of the key features

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.990
of conceptual understanding,

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.450
and I'll talk more about that shortly.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.500
And finally, concerning the
constitutive normativity

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.840
of the conceptual domain,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.740
which he, in the end,

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.830
understands in terms of the inseparability

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.840
of matter and mattering.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.760
So I'm gonna go through
those four themes first,

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.240
but then I'm going to raise
four primary questions

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.610
in response to Brian's talk.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.380
First, I wanna pick up on the question

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.130
of whether his initial
characterization of a feature-space

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:24.780
is the right way to think about

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.210
the phenomena he's talking about.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:31.230
Second, I'm gonna ask a
question about whether the world

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.640
that we inhabit is in the non-conceptual,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.050
given the pervasive involvement
of conceptual practices,

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.840
languages, imagery, equipment, music,

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.493
and so forth, in the world.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.960
Third, I'm going to look a little harder

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.320
at Brian's conception of the conceptual,

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.380
oh, and ask whether instead of starting

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.050
from the kinds of conceptual structures

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.373
that say, someone like,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.590
Evans, is talking about.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.300
Whether we should talk start

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.520
from "conceptuality in the wild,"

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.370
much as Brian's work on computation,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.280
suggests we start with conceptual,

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.650
with computation in the wild.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.660
And finally, I wanna
ask a little bit about

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.200
the place of other agents
in the world we inhabit

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.360
and their bearing on the framing

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.230
and grounding of conceptual normativity.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:26.943
So, first theme,

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.633
in Brian's ontological
account of intentionality

00:06:31.633 --> 00:06:32.483
&amp; contentfulness.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.070
As most accounts of this tend
to be what he calls epistemic.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.530
That is, they begin with an account

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:43.110
of what I would call "empty" content,

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.810
how someone takes the world to be.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.367
And then, only then, I ask whether

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:50.340
and how that content is fulfilled.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.320
And that's why epistemic accounts,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.400
target objects as instantiating
properties and relations

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:57.933
and grouped in the settings,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.993
into sets accordingly.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.800
The ontological understanding
of the conceptual

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.710
that Brian is interested in,

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.980
starts with involvement in the world,

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.790
and then asks how aspects of it

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.937
are takings-of "something as something."

00:07:19.111 --> 00:07:20.250
And the difference is

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.590
that the epistemic account presumes

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.570
that the something is objects,

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:29.550
and we take it as having
properties is and relations.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.480
And Brian's account

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.000
is taking the world as articulated

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.000
into property objects and properties.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.303
And hence, in doing so,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.470
both objects &amp; properties
are in the second something

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.600
on the far side of the "as."

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.340
And that's why Brian thinks

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:58.260
that epistemic accounts are
actually implicitly ontological

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.200
that is committed to taking the world

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.110
as already articulated into objects.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.860
And that's what he's, of
course, putting into question.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.843
The second theme,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.120
which I'll be very brief about,

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.830
because we're,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.683
is that, Brian thinks that the concept,

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.940
there's a kind of fine-grained richness

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.980
to the world that we encounter.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.830
And that this is vindicated
in multiple ways.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:28.770
First, by ordinary experience,

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:31.980
whose content and colors and the speed,

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.440
at which we drive around a corner,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.203
and various other phenomena are,

00:08:42.546 --> 00:08:44.760
you know, have a kind
of fine-grained richness

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.400
that outruns our conceptualizations.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.450
He also thinks that's true
of scientific understanding

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.830
from the physics of fields,

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.020
which are not discreetly bounded,

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.510
to the practicalities of cartography,

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.450
which have to give boundaries to islands,

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.550
mountains, forest, coastlines,

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.080
and so forth,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.460
where there's no exactly
determinant boundary to that.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:09.930
But first and foremost,

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:11.370
I think, for Brian,

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.113
this is vindicated by the
history of the computational

00:09:14.113 --> 00:09:15.900
&amp; cognitive sciences.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.670
First, by the failings

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.820
of good old-fashioned
artificial intelligence.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.743
Second, by the,

00:09:24.037 --> 00:09:26.010
(paper rustling)

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:29.610
you know, the practical challenges
of real-world computation

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.180
and especially now by the
comparative success compared

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.070
to GOFAI of machine-learning
&amp; 2nd-wave AI.

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:40.830
I agree on all three of
these stories, all errors,

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:43.320
but I will say something more shortly

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.213
about how we characterize it.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.730
The third theme,

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:50.400
some of the Aspects of
Conceptual Understanding

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.203
that figure in Brian's account.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.890
First, that conceptual
understanding abstracts

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:59.970
from the diverse actual or
possible particular realizations

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.730
of the same conceptual content.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:06.450
Second, that conceptual
understanding allows

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:09.060
the recombine ability of its concepts

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.830
in various predicative,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:13.770
disjunctive or hypothetical judgments,

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.740
and in alethic, deontic,
&amp; temporal modalities,

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.770
and various other reformulations.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.080
A third point, which he said less about,

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.720
that Brian emphasizes
in a number of places

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.560
is what he calls,

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.810
would call, the causal non-effectiveness

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:30.450
of conceptual representation.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.930
That my representing something

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.780
by itself is non-consequential.

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:41.610
And that it is also as such
indiscernible by others.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.177
As he puts it at one point,

00:10:43.177 --> 00:10:44.730
"You can't build an app

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.530
that will ping every time someone

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.410
in the world thinks about me," right?

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:53.100
It doesn't have that
kind of detectability.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.850
Now it's important to realize
that this is one place

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:01.410
where Brian's ontological
conception really matters,

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.790
because if you take conceptualization

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:10.020
to be about a non-conceptual world,

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.300
then on an epistemic account,

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.790
that's gonna lead to a
kind of global skepticism

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.460
or a global disconnection, right?

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.080
That, you know,

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.843
we would be misrepresenting the world.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.540
For Brian, that's not the case,

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.630
because we're already
in touch with the world.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:31.950
And that to be genuinely
intentional and conceptual,

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:33.780
that conceptual understanding

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:38.780
then has to be concretized
back in action or application

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.250
what he calls "filling in"

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:44.460
such that the conceptual
content that's extracted from

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.180
then has to be filled
in in a concrete case,

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:51.813
to which we apply what we do.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:58.580
And finally, the fourth
sort of initial theme

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:03.300
is The Normativity of
Intentional Directedness.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:06.540
This is something that's
pretty widely familiar

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:10.020
and indeed is the source
of familiar concern

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:14.010
to naturalize intentionality
or conceptual understanding

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.560
by explaining how physical systems

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.830
that instantiate conceptual
can be answerable

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.213
to conceptual norms.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:26.850
Traditional approaches
to this question start

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.610
with what Brian calls "statical norms"

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.520
truth, reference, and the like, right?

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:35.850
And as the target of either naturalization

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.220
or for those so inclined

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:41.940
as the target of non-natural
determination by reason.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.790
And from that account
of the statical norms,

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:47.190
it then builds dynamical norms

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.473
of appropriate uses and inferences.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:54.390
Brian's experiences in the
trenches of computer science,

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.820
cognitive science and AI,

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.300
suggests that that
strategy is broken back.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.690
The dynamical norms of practice and use,

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:07.680
govern the statical norms
of truth and reference.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:08.910
And this he points out,

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:11.610
has a very major consequence.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:12.780
In that case,

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:15.930
something else must
determine the dynamical norm,

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:20.490
something other than merely
preserving the statical norms

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.563
of truth and reference.

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:26.940
Brian's much more
extensive response to this

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:28.530
in his papers than in the talk

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:31.140
is important, but unconventional,

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.443
in terms of the normativity of ontology.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:40.230
For Brian, the dual sense
of matter and mattering,

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.960
of material ontology,

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.280
and material arguments or
material considerations

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:48.420
is no coincidence,

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:49.770
but, is in fact,

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:51.903
integral to what it is to be material.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:56.130
He further specifies ontology

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:59.520
as the locust of a kind
of natural normativity.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:01.170
This is now quoting from the paper

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:03.360
from which the talk is drawn.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.450
Which abstractions a creature makes,

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:09.990
and thus, what objects
there are for that creature,

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:13.620
arises out of the constituently
norm-governed life

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.303
that that creature leads.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:20.190
Objects, themselves,
exist in world's governed

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:25.170
by significance,
interpretation, and importance.

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:26.460
This is, I think,

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.540
a broadly biological
conception of normativity.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:34.710
And it frames Brian's account
of truth as what is at issue

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:37.623
and at stake in conceptual activity.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:41.460
Conceptual judgments are true

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:44.130
and the inferences we make are good.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.230
If so taking them,

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.630
and fulfills the normative concerns

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:51.240
that governs a conceptual systems,

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.863
projects and practice.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:57.510
Now, Brian notes and then rejects,

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:02.070
the widespread appeal to
evolutionary adaptation

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:05.490
to provide the relevant dynamical norms.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.140
And he thinks that's the case

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:07.973
because he thinks that

00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:12.973
it doesn't provide sufficient
normative articulation.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:17.850
We need not just norms for
maintaining a way of life

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:21.570
and in terms of evolutionary survival,

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:24.300
but for sustaining a meaningful life,

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.270
a morally significant life.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:31.980
And these more richly
differentiated normative concerns,

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:35.460
govern not only ethics,
politics and aesthetics,

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.620
but also ontology.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:41.040
How the world we inhabit and answer to,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:45.390
ought to be conceptualized
in living such lives?

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.070
So those four themes,

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:52.070
I think, are things that Brian
and I largely agree about.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.600
They are also fairly unconventional,

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:57.540
even provocative in the
context of most thinking

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:58.623
on these subjects.

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:03.213
Even though,

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.720
you know, both our views are grounded in

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:07.590
and on the one hand,

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:09.390
in Brian's work on the cognitive

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.610
and computational sciences,

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:15.210
both of our work in
interdisciplinary science studies,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.120
and my work in the philosophy
of scientific practice,

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:21.243
evolutionary biology and social theory.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.260
Despite that agreement and placement

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:28.560
on one edge of contemporary debates,

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.750
its, I do have some questions

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:34.920
that point to consequential
differences in how we develop

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:38.700
and justify these provocative thesis.

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:40.530
I will have to be brief,

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.980
I've written about them
elsewhere at some length,

00:16:44.242 --> 00:16:46.890
in both published and forthcoming work.

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:51.780
So the first question
concerns his initials,

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:53.940
as part of speculative characterization

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:58.940
of the Non-Conceptual World
as a Space of Features.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.620
Whereas, underlying this,

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:04.020
there's a tension in Brian's project

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:08.370
that is trying to give a conceptual grasp

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:12.930
of a Non-Conceptual
World as Non-Conceptual.

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:15.570
And elsewhere, he recognizes that,

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:19.620
for example, in the artificial
intelligence book by talking,

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.940
by giving a demonstrative
indication of the world as that,

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:27.090
which is registered conceptually,

00:17:27.090 --> 00:17:28.470
but can only be explicate

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:31.950
in further conceptual explications.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.230
I have a couple of worries
though about the move

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:35.670
to feature space.

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:38.430
One is, that many of the
kinds of things Brian

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:42.750
is suggesting might be Features
of a Non-Conceptual World

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.820
are actually interactive aspects

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:46.920
of our engagement with the world.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:51.030
I think of Kathleen Akins's
classic paper on thermoreceptors

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:52.563
as not getting the,

00:17:53.565 --> 00:17:54.900
or as an objective feature,

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:59.850
but how temperature
matters to us or colors

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:02.820
as neither objective features of the world

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.680
nor subjective qualia.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:08.760
And, perhaps, above all of
Karen Barad's conception

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:10.530
of objects is not an in,

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:12.690
as not intrinsic to the world,

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:14.943
but rather as intra-active.

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:19.560
And hence, a causal conception of things.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.600
There's a deeper point as well,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:23.220
and that is, that, you know,

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:27.150
Brian distinguishes
perceptual registration

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:31.530
of a Non-Conceptual World in conceptually,

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.990
and then doing conceptual work,

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.600
and then practically filling-in this.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.280
Whereas, I think,

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:43.280
that perception and action
are always going on together.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.560
All perception involves
practical activity,

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.860
all action is also
perceptually responsive,

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:53.490
and our engagement with that

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:58.350
and the world we are in is
densely causally entangled.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:18:59.880
So that's gonna be my first question.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:02.740
The relation between causal
entanglement or features

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:07.450
as a way of getting at

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:10.850
the richness of the
world that we agree on.

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:14.760
Second, I wanna ask a
question whether the world

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:19.760
that we inhabit actually
is in the non-conceptual?

00:19:20.677 --> 00:19:23.850
And that's because the
fine-grained richness

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.460
of the worlds we live in

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.140
that we both agree about

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:31.230
is also crosscut with ostensions,

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:35.520
words, images, functional spaces,

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.743
equipment, and social roles.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:43.760
These elements have a kind
of dual character, right?

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:46.290
Or that is they are also part

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:50.790
of the richly entangled
fabric of the world

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:54.603
and they are also
conceptually articulative.

00:19:56.040 --> 00:20:00.000
I think, we need things
like Adam Lowe's paintings

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.400
and machine-learning algorithms

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:07.400
to abstract from our
pervasive concrete engagement

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.073
with our conceptually
articulative practices.

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:15.400
Phonemic articulation actually exemplifies

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:18.483
this duality, right?

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.490
We know that the phonemes people utter,

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:27.330
are continuous and
indistinguishable and yet,

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:30.090
but they also function discreetly

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:32.463
to mark out distinct phonemes.

00:20:34.053 --> 00:20:37.260
And so part of what
I'm suggesting here is,

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:38.520
along with Brian,

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:43.290
that a conceptually
articulated world is not found,

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:44.940
but we nevertheless live in it

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:47.583
as already made by our practices.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:54.500
The third issue, concerns
whether we should start instead

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:58.320
with "conceptuality in the wild,"

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.860
following on Ed Hutchinson's
"Navigation in the Wild"

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:05.100
and Brian's talk about
computation in the wild.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:10.050
And I note, that despite
prioritizing dynamical norms,

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.840
Brian starts with a characterization

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:15.783
of conceptuality as statical.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:19.350
We only get to inferences

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.780
as connecting determinate conceptual,

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.870
more or less determinate
conceptual contents.

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:28.950
I wanna talk about
conceptuality "in the wild"

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:32.250
that is in our conceptual practices,

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:35.550
and that starts with material inference

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:38.460
as integral to conceptual contents.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:43.150
The content of a representation involves

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.980
its appropriate inferential connections

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:49.293
and with others.

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:53.160
So that is,

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:55.530
second, in that context,

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:58.890
our actual conceptual
engagement in the world,

00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:01.740
definitely includes the conceptual role

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:03.690
of things like indexicals,

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.060
demonstratives, anaphora,

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:10.680
and their iconic and
expressive equivalence,

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:15.680
including the ways we negotiate
conceptual disagreements

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:20.310
via ostension, indirect
quotation, interpretation,

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.353
and so forth, right?

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.233
So I want to,

00:22:27.699 --> 00:22:28.740
in this respect,

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:32.370
conceptual understanding
is not under our control.

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:35.520
The ways we articulate
the world conceptually

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:38.130
and assess its normative significance

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:41.940
is continually negotiated
with other agencies.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.020
Human, to be sure,

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:45.303
but I would also argue non-human.

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:48.513
And like McDowell for the,

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:53.010
actually, it's going back
to the indexical's point.

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:54.570
I share with McDowell,

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:58.140
this emphasis on indexicals as conceptual,

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:00.510
but for very different reasons.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:02.310
Finally, then I would add

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:04.980
that part of why I think GOFAI failed

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:08.010
is not merely that it didn't get at

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:09.480
the richness of the world,

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:11.880
but it also abstracts from the richness

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.970
and complexity of our conceptual life.

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:16.800
And so, the moral I would draw,

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:21.143
is the conceptuality has to
start with the dynamics of use

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:26.613
and not with the static
structures of conceptual systems.

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:33.110
My final question, reconsiders
normativity, evolution,

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:36.003
and the social ecological
world that we inhabit.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:39.510
Well, I completely agree with Brian

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.570
in invoking the normative
complexity of human lives

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:45.450
and enhance the inadequacy of appeals

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:49.140
to simply to evolutionary adaptation.

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:52.560
Now, he understandably doesn't say much

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:56.220
about the normativity
involved here in detail.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.443
He did more than enough for one talk.

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:03.540
But I note that he does seem to want

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:05.850
to begin with the ontology

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:09.180
of our conceptual
engagement with the world,

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:13.200
before situating it in a
broader normative space.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:15.963
And I just think, that
in the end doesn't work.

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:21.570
Evolutionary biology,

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:26.490
in its traditional focus
on adaptation, won't do.

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.970
But evolutionary biology now
gives us much richer resources

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:32.490
for understanding dynamical norms.

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:33.690
In terms of organism,

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:38.220
environment, entanglement,
ecological developmental biology,

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.100
and niche construction, among others.

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:44.250
And so, looking to the
end of Brian's talk,

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:46.950
I think we need to ask how

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:50.970
and why our practices engage the world

00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:54.210
at spacial temporal modal distances

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:57.330
for which its conceptual abstraction

00:24:57.330 --> 00:25:00.270
from current situations is useful.

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:05.100
I think, both the need for
long distance understanding

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:08.130
and the possibility
and normative authority

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:12.240
of our conceptual practices
emerged first through

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.640
the evolution of a cooperative,

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.760
inter-cooperatively interdependent way

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.700
of life among early prominence.

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:21.900
And that in turn,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:26.610
enabled our current practice
differentiated way of life.

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:30.600
The practices we each take up here and now

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:33.660
are closely interdependent
with other practices

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:35.400
and ways of life

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:39.150
that nevertheless mostly
take place at a distance.

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:41.610
And it's our need to sustain

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:45.450
that cooperative interdependence
among diverse practices

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:50.400
in practitioners is why
conceptual capacities evolved

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:54.810
and persist as long haul trucks &amp; highways

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:57.570
for engaging with the world at a distance

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:59.610
and more abstractly.

00:25:59.610 --> 00:26:03.903
That, however, has to be a
story for another occasion.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:09.570
- Great.

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:13.860
Thank you so much, Joe, for that fantastic

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:17.970
and clearly laying out
the points of agreement

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:21.990
and the places where you have questions

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:25.920
that were raised by
Brian's talk yesterday.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:28.650
And so, Brian, now, you have a few moments

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:31.030
to engage with him before

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:37.100
we have our questions
readied up for both of you.

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:40.403
- Okay, great.

00:26:40.403 --> 00:26:42.000
Well, listen, thank you, Joe.

00:26:43.053 --> 00:26:44.160
This is a fabulous,

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:46.290
in fact, clearer, I think,

00:26:46.290 --> 00:26:47.730
especially up to the interlude,

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:49.590
clearer articulation
of what I wanted to say

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.540
than I myself said it.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:52.980
I wanna make a note,

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:55.860
a kind of nod to John Hoagland,

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:56.700
the late John Hoagland,

00:26:56.700 --> 00:27:01.700
the philosopher extraordinaire
that Joe and I both knew.

00:27:01.770 --> 00:27:04.683
When Hoagland and I would
talk late in tonight often,

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:07.927
we developed a habit of
saying to each other,

00:27:07.927 --> 00:27:10.830
"That's what I was just about to think."

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:14.850
And some of what you have done, Joe, here,

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:15.683
is I think,

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:21.750
you've earned this moniker,

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:24.450
I think, of respect and agreement of,

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:27.532
you know, yes, that's what
I was just about to think.

00:27:27.532 --> 00:27:28.442
So, thank you for that

00:27:28.442 --> 00:27:30.453
and for that clear articulation.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:33.003
I wanna say a couple of things.

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:38.340
I'm not quite sure in what order,

00:27:41.285 --> 00:27:42.118
but maybe this.

00:27:42.118 --> 00:27:45.390
I think maybe one of the most
important things going on

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:48.900
in your response and my
characterization is that

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:53.900
I do lean a little bit
towards Evan's conception

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:55.653
of conceptuality.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:58.980
And my sense is you are of a medallion

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:01.560
and I actually am more of
a medallion myself as well

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:04.350
in the sense that I kind of
agree more deeply with McDowell.

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:06.900
I think he's taking on the issue

00:28:06.900 --> 00:28:10.233
of being engaged with the world cut deep.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:14.430
So, you, I think,

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.500
this is fair to say,

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:18.390
especially, for example,
in your third point

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:20.733
of dealing with
conceptuality in the world,

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:23.280
sorry, in the wild.

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:24.300
Of course, in the world too,

00:28:24.300 --> 00:28:25.533
but in the wild.

00:28:27.090 --> 00:28:30.030
I think the kind of conceptuality
that you're getting at

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:31.830
is what would be going on in the wild,

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:34.050
might include things that I wouldn't,

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:36.720
in my talk, at least,
have called conceptual.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.035
Now, there is-

00:28:38.035 --> 00:28:39.790
- My question's absolutely right.

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:42.240
- There is an issue as to
how we should use this word

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:45.130
because one possible question
I could have got yesterday

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:47.520
and I was wondering if I get it

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:49.477
and nobody actually asked
it is the following,

00:28:49.477 --> 00:28:50.523
"Look, Brian,

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:53.500
your sense of..."

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:54.540
I remember I said yesterday

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.360
that the conception of the
ridge didn't turn the ridge into

00:28:57.360 --> 00:28:59.280
a sort of conceptual object.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.563
It was still the rich object that it was.

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:05.047
Somebody might have asked me,

00:29:05.047 --> 00:29:07.170
"Look, is there any conceptual ontology

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:10.200
or is that just a sort
of theoretical fantasy

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:12.420
that people have used as a
way of explaining actually

00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:13.647
the content of language
and so on and so forth."

00:29:13.647 --> 00:29:17.070
And I actually think it's a mistake.

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:20.790
There actually isn't any that
human intentionality never

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:22.470
is fully conceptual,

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:24.270
in the sense, in Brian's,

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:26.403
sorry, sense of conceptuality.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:29.670
Certainly, I think,

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:33.093
and I think this matters
to the discussion.

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:37.110
I do not think that all of our thought

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:41.490
or our use of language
is in fact conceptual.

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:43.500
Remember I talked about the poets,

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:46.380
I talked about that
quote from Henry James,

