To say that there's more than I know,

00:55:23.010 --> 00:55:26.133
I know that there's more
than I know there to be is,

00:55:27.090 --> 00:55:28.290
you know, that's kind of,

00:55:28.290 --> 00:55:29.730
I mean, that's a challenge, right?

00:55:29.730 --> 00:55:31.530
- Yeah, there's another
layer wrapped up there.

00:55:31.530 --> 00:55:34.063
- There's another layer there.

00:55:34.063 --> 00:55:36.600
I guess, my sense of what has mattered

00:55:36.600 --> 00:55:38.400
to me about people I've known,

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:40.470
just to be very personal about this,

00:55:40.470 --> 00:55:41.463
is actually,

00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:45.640
a shared deference

00:55:46.500 --> 00:55:49.420
to that which is beyond

00:55:51.660 --> 00:55:56.610
rich patterns of causal
intra-action and engagement

00:55:56.610 --> 00:55:57.540
and so on and so forth.

00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:58.410
I just,

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:01.353
I find the restriction to,

00:56:04.161 --> 00:56:05.280
I mean, you're absolutely right,

00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:09.030
extremely rich and extremely
being better understood

00:56:09.030 --> 00:56:10.980
all the time and so on and so forth.

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:12.840
Patterns of causal interaction,

00:56:12.840 --> 00:56:14.880
I think, and casual engagement,

00:56:14.880 --> 00:56:16.020
and all of this kind of stuff.

00:56:16.020 --> 00:56:17.880
I mean, the stuff that
the scientific revolution

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:19.380
actually opened our eyes too.

00:56:19.380 --> 00:56:20.213
I think,

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:24.360
it just feels to me like a projection

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:25.770
onto the wall of the cave

00:56:25.770 --> 00:56:30.770
and that no matter how
rich the shadows are,

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:35.880
that there was something

00:56:35.880 --> 00:56:38.460
to the scientific revolution's claim

00:56:38.460 --> 00:56:41.520
that we can explain the
universe in these terms,

00:56:41.520 --> 00:56:43.650
even if they had no
idea about the resources

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:45.160
of revolutionary biology now

00:56:46.098 --> 00:56:46.931
and so on and so forth,

00:56:46.931 --> 00:56:48.993
the niche construction and God knows what.

00:56:49.920 --> 00:56:53.140
I think it's still a shadow of that

00:56:55.050 --> 00:56:56.970
to which we defer.

00:56:56.970 --> 00:56:58.680
- Right, mm-hmm.

00:56:58.680 --> 00:57:01.830
- As I think it is part of what that,

00:57:01.830 --> 00:57:03.270
to which we refer.

00:57:03.270 --> 00:57:04.167
- Well, of course, I agree with that.

00:57:04.167 --> 00:57:08.400
- And it only gets its sense as an ongoing

00:57:08.400 --> 00:57:13.350
and open-ended articulation
of that, right?

00:57:13.350 --> 00:57:14.613
Because, I mean, the two things-

00:57:14.613 --> 00:57:16.200
- But, right, no, we
would agree with that,

00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:17.430
but the question is,

00:57:17.430 --> 00:57:21.160
is that open-ended exploration

00:57:23.160 --> 00:57:28.160
open to any understanding we get of it,

00:57:28.569 --> 00:57:32.640
or is it closed methodologically

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:35.400
because of a commitment
to causal explanation?

00:57:35.400 --> 00:57:37.530
And I think if it's the former,

00:57:37.530 --> 00:57:38.850
then there's nothing to say.

00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:39.750
- I wouldn't say it's closed,

00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:41.610
but it's restricted, right?

00:57:41.610 --> 00:57:43.170
If you have a commitment to causal.

00:57:43.170 --> 00:57:44.003
- Yeah.

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:46.890
- Well, if you haven't
restricted the causal,

00:57:46.890 --> 00:57:48.480
then I think you're not
gonna explain things

00:57:48.480 --> 00:57:51.960
that in fact, the fundamental
constituent regularities

00:57:51.960 --> 00:57:53.040
of which are not causal.

00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:54.720
And I think reference is one thing,

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:55.563
the fundamental,

00:57:57.060 --> 00:57:59.520
the fundamental regularities
of which are not causal.

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:01.470
That's I think the importance
of that impossible-

00:58:01.470 --> 00:58:03.753
- And, see, here's,

00:58:04.740 --> 00:58:06.540
I think this is where,

00:58:06.540 --> 00:58:09.060
there's actually more
agreement than you think.

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:11.030
Because reference,

00:58:11.030 --> 00:58:14.460
in the sense in which you mean it, okay?

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:18.060
So the way in which
you can utter sentences

00:58:18.060 --> 00:58:22.383
that refer to things that we
are causally disconnected with,

00:58:23.490 --> 00:58:25.470
I have no problem with, okay?

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:26.610
We do that,

00:58:26.610 --> 00:58:27.603
that's one of the,

00:58:28.650 --> 00:58:32.610
next question, how do we do that, right?

00:58:32.610 --> 00:58:37.610
And, you know, this is where
I think the dynamical norms

00:58:38.280 --> 00:58:40.980
of conceptual articulation

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:45.090
and the ways in which
the concepts themselves

00:58:45.090 --> 00:58:49.950
get their content from how
we engage with the world,

00:58:49.950 --> 00:58:51.933
how we engage with one another.

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:55.020
- But-
- The concepts relate

00:58:55.020 --> 00:58:57.330
to one another inferentially,

00:58:57.330 --> 00:59:00.750
which allows you to express new things.

00:59:00.750 --> 00:59:03.570
Maybe you have to write a novel

00:59:03.570 --> 00:59:07.890
in order to develop the concepts to point

00:59:07.890 --> 00:59:09.330
to, you know, without,

00:59:09.330 --> 00:59:12.090
of often what novelists are doing, right?

00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:15.630
But they're thereby allowing
us to refer to things

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:17.820
that we previously couldn't refer to.

00:59:17.820 --> 00:59:18.990
- Well, I think there's truth to that,

00:59:18.990 --> 00:59:19.823
but the problem is,

00:59:19.823 --> 00:59:22.023
to argue that they do that in virtue,

00:59:24.030 --> 00:59:27.630
rich causal engagements
and stuff is circular.

00:59:27.630 --> 00:59:29.580
Because, in fact, if the
reference of the novel

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:31.650
is something that rich patterns

00:59:31.650 --> 00:59:33.150
of causal engagement can't explain,

00:59:33.150 --> 00:59:34.590
then in fact, what it is
that they're referring to

00:59:34.590 --> 00:59:35.820
can't be explained that way.

00:59:35.820 --> 00:59:36.653
The thing is,

00:59:43.110 --> 00:59:45.420
my sense, I mean, this is
the McDowell part of you

00:59:45.420 --> 00:59:47.610
I think that I think is actually
a little bit incompatible

00:59:47.610 --> 00:59:48.960
with the Barad part of you.

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:51.803
One of the senses I have of McDowell

00:59:51.803 --> 00:59:54.210
is that part of what it is
to be conceptual for him

00:59:54.210 --> 00:59:55.800
is that it is in fact part

00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:58.110
of a conception of the world, right?

00:59:58.110 --> 00:59:59.460
And McDowell is not anyone

00:59:59.460 --> 01:00:01.530
who's lost any sleep
over not being compatible

01:00:01.530 --> 01:00:02.977
with any scientific results.

01:00:02.977 --> 01:00:04.186
(Joseph laughs)

01:00:04.186 --> 01:00:05.640
(Brian chuckles)

01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:06.473
That's one of the virtues of being

01:00:06.473 --> 01:00:08.200
a Pittsburgh philosophy department.

01:00:09.960 --> 01:00:11.880
But the thing is, the
conception of the world,

01:00:11.880 --> 01:00:13.470
holding things accountable

01:00:13.470 --> 01:00:14.730
to being part of the world, right?

01:00:14.730 --> 01:00:16.440
Which is something
McDowell feels, I think,

01:00:16.440 --> 01:00:19.530
and something Hoagland
actually believed in deeply.

01:00:19.530 --> 01:00:22.980
I think that conception
of being a whole world,

01:00:22.980 --> 01:00:24.810
that which is one that beyond,

01:00:24.810 --> 01:00:27.003
which there isn't anything
and so on and so forth.

01:00:27.003 --> 01:00:29.580
I don't think that can be explained

01:00:29.580 --> 01:00:32.880
in terms of rich patterns of causal.

01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:34.890
- Okay, so I think at this point

01:00:34.890 --> 01:00:38.250
we should probably
invite if Professor Barad

01:00:38.250 --> 01:00:41.430
is interested in commenting.

01:00:41.430 --> 01:00:43.110
- Absolutely, and also next year

01:00:43.110 --> 01:00:44.520
you have to invite us all to bring

01:00:44.520 --> 01:00:46.369
the novels we're gonna write.

01:00:46.369 --> 01:00:49.933
- (laughs) Well, not me. (laughs)

01:00:51.480 --> 01:00:53.264
- Sorry, Priya, I didn't
mean to interrupted you.

01:00:53.264 --> 01:00:55.063
- No, not at all.

01:00:55.063 --> 01:00:58.430
I have invited Professor Barad to...

01:01:00.660 --> 01:01:03.750
- [Karen] Well, hi,
thanks for inviting me.

01:01:03.750 --> 01:01:05.130
- Hi.

01:01:05.130 --> 01:01:06.300
- Oh, hi, Karen.
- Lovely to see you.

01:01:06.300 --> 01:01:07.860
- [Karen] Thanks to you both,

01:01:07.860 --> 01:01:11.400
for this amazing exchange.

01:01:11.400 --> 01:01:14.250
I'm just gonna be super brief by saying

01:01:14.250 --> 01:01:16.440
that I don't know how
much Joe is a Baradian,

01:01:16.440 --> 01:01:18.454
but I'm certainly a Rousean.

01:01:18.454 --> 01:01:20.640
(Priya laughs)
And I think that he's,

01:01:20.640 --> 01:01:23.373
I think that Joe has done a marvelous job,

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:27.630
addressing Brian's objections about this.

01:01:27.630 --> 01:01:29.400
And so I'm just gonna leave it at that.

01:01:29.400 --> 01:01:30.233
Thanks.

01:01:31.080 --> 01:01:32.640
- Thank you so much, Karen.

01:01:32.640 --> 01:01:34.110
It was such-
- Thank you, Karen.

01:01:34.110 --> 01:01:36.155
I had no idea you were here, so.

01:01:36.155 --> 01:01:37.200
(Joseph laughs)
- Otherwise,

01:01:37.200 --> 01:01:39.354
you wouldn't have talked about
me drinking Kool-Aid, I bet.

01:01:39.354 --> 01:01:40.301
(Priya laughs)
(Joseph laughs)

01:01:40.301 --> 01:01:41.310
- I would not have.

01:01:41.310 --> 01:01:44.262
- No, we'll bring you
in on the whiskey, K.

01:01:44.262 --> 01:01:45.495
(Karen laughs)

01:01:45.495 --> 01:01:47.430
(Brian chuckles)
That will be much more fun.

01:01:47.430 --> 01:01:49.019
- [Karen] I look forward to that, yeah.

01:01:49.019 --> 01:01:49.852
(Joseph laughs)
(Brian chuckles)

01:01:49.852 --> 01:01:50.685
- That'll be great.

01:01:50.685 --> 01:01:51.690
The first round's on me.

01:01:52.530 --> 01:01:53.400
- Karen, thank you so much.

01:01:53.400 --> 01:01:54.547
- [Karen] Yeah.

01:01:54.547 --> 01:01:55.440
- Okay.

01:01:55.440 --> 01:01:58.020
I mean, I guess I have one quick question,

01:01:58.020 --> 01:01:59.010
but I think I'll wait

01:01:59.010 --> 01:02:02.910
and I'll turn it over to
people in the audience today.

01:02:02.910 --> 01:02:06.247
And I think June Lao has a question,

01:02:06.247 --> 01:02:09.420
"It seems that under Brian's
conception of both conceptual

01:02:09.420 --> 01:02:12.060
and non-conceptual forms of normativity,

01:02:12.060 --> 01:02:14.310
there is no reduction to causation,

01:02:14.310 --> 01:02:16.500
evolution, or practice.

01:02:16.500 --> 01:02:19.380
Does the debate here may
not be about conceptual

01:02:19.380 --> 01:02:21.000
versus non-conceptual per se,

01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:24.180
but about the constitution of normativity?

01:02:24.180 --> 01:02:25.500
By Brian's conception,

01:02:25.500 --> 01:02:29.010
the non-conceptual world
plays an irreducible role

01:02:29.010 --> 01:02:31.200
in the constitution of normativity,

01:02:31.200 --> 01:02:32.790
conceptual or non-conceptual,

01:02:32.790 --> 01:02:36.540
and that aspect is not
captured by causal evolutionary

01:02:36.540 --> 01:02:38.280
and practical accounts.

01:02:38.280 --> 01:02:39.357
Is that correct?"

01:02:40.200 --> 01:02:43.950
Yeah, I mean, I think
it's my understanding too,

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:45.881
but I could have missed something.

01:02:45.881 --> 01:02:48.660
- But I think, June's comment
is actually interesting.

01:02:48.660 --> 01:02:50.340
The last sentence I think is,

01:02:50.340 --> 01:02:52.020
it's too easy to answer it

01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:53.250
in the way in which it's framed.

01:02:53.250 --> 01:02:55.500
So I wanna sort of raise the anti,

01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:59.523
and then also agree with
the raised anti-version.

01:03:01.565 --> 01:03:02.940
Is it captured by causal evolutionary

01:03:02.940 --> 01:03:04.080
and practical accounts?

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:05.640
I think that's correct,

01:03:05.640 --> 01:03:06.663
it is not.

01:03:07.500 --> 01:03:09.450
But I would say something stronger,

01:03:09.450 --> 01:03:11.850
which is that the
constitution of normativity

01:03:11.850 --> 01:03:13.470
in the human case,

01:03:13.470 --> 01:03:15.150
and I don't know about
other biological animals.

01:03:15.150 --> 01:03:16.230
I think this might be a place

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:18.720
we actually have sort of transcended

01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:19.920
our biological heritage.

01:03:21.450 --> 01:03:22.800
The constitution of normativity,

01:03:22.800 --> 01:03:25.800
conceptuality and
non-conceptuality in the human case

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:27.720
is not captured by causal evolutionary

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:29.253
or practical phenomena.

01:03:31.170 --> 01:03:32.790
So in other words, it's
not just the accounts,

01:03:32.790 --> 01:03:34.770
it's what those accounts are accounts of

01:03:34.770 --> 01:03:36.900
I think, that is not enough.

01:03:36.900 --> 01:03:39.481
So yes, I agree with
you and raise you one.

01:03:39.481 --> 01:03:40.926
(Joseph chuckles)

01:03:40.926 --> 01:03:41.880
- Great.

01:03:41.880 --> 01:03:44.583
Thank you so much, June,
for that great question.

01:03:46.560 --> 01:03:48.510
So as we wait for more people

01:03:48.510 --> 01:03:50.730
to chime in with their questions,

01:03:50.730 --> 01:03:53.010
so I wanted a clarification, right?

01:03:53.010 --> 01:03:56.160
So, and the clarification
is about the negotiation

01:03:56.160 --> 01:03:59.250
of conceptual disagreements, right?

01:03:59.250 --> 01:04:00.083
- Yeah.

01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:06.750
- So, is it that the arbitration

01:04:06.750 --> 01:04:11.750
or negotiation between
different conceptual accounts

01:04:13.200 --> 01:04:17.400
of reality is not actually
challenging per se

01:04:17.400 --> 01:04:18.450
at the end of the day

01:04:18.450 --> 01:04:22.050
because you share a
lexicon and a language,

01:04:22.050 --> 01:04:25.923
or are there incommensurable,

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:29.400
not that I wanna sort of evoke June here,

01:04:29.400 --> 01:04:30.750
but, you know.

01:04:30.750 --> 01:04:31.583
- Neither one,

01:04:31.583 --> 01:04:34.169
and I think this is an
important question, I think.

01:04:34.169 --> 01:04:36.336
Because what I tend to do,

01:04:37.697 --> 01:04:39.930
I think, would tend to do with
the consensual disagreement

01:04:39.930 --> 01:04:43.770
is I would say, "Look,
come over from here."

01:04:43.770 --> 01:04:44.940
Let's just sit down

01:04:44.940 --> 01:04:47.280
or maybe we're gonna go
walk around the field

01:04:47.280 --> 01:04:48.903
and, you know, and...

01:04:48.903 --> 01:04:50.490
Now, why?

01:04:50.490 --> 01:04:54.000
Because having dinner and
walking around the field

01:04:54.000 --> 01:04:56.780
and having libations in
common and so on and so forth,

01:04:56.780 --> 01:04:58.530
is a form of engagement

01:04:58.530 --> 01:05:01.590
that is not entirely within
the conceptual realm.

01:05:01.590 --> 01:05:03.510
And I don't think

01:05:03.510 --> 01:05:07.350
that sharing of vocabulary,

01:05:07.350 --> 01:05:09.420
I mean, we do share vocabulary
in some sense, right?

01:05:09.420 --> 01:05:13.020
I know this word conceptual, for example.

01:05:13.020 --> 01:05:13.853
And,

01:05:15.420 --> 01:05:16.980
you know, it's different across languages.

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:19.195
I mean, it's interesting
that in French, I believe,

01:05:19.195 --> 01:05:22.110
(speaks French) is the French
translation of two words

01:05:22.110 --> 01:05:25.410
in English, "Conscience
and consciousness."

01:05:25.410 --> 01:05:26.550
And I have asked people

01:05:26.550 --> 01:05:27.780
who are native French speakers,

01:05:27.780 --> 01:05:29.643
whether they actually have,

01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:33.180
French makes only one notion such that it,

01:05:33.180 --> 01:05:35.207
we, English, have pulled
it apart or what's going

01:05:35.207 --> 01:05:36.600
and so on and so forth.

01:05:36.600 --> 01:05:38.250
And I've had different answers.

01:05:38.250 --> 01:05:40.740
Some French people that are
trained in analytic philosophy

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:41.573
and stuff, think, "Look,

01:05:41.573 --> 01:05:43.440
it's just a word that's
used for both things

01:05:43.440 --> 01:05:45.120
and no French person is confused."

01:05:45.120 --> 01:05:46.177
And I've had other French people say,

01:05:46.177 --> 01:05:47.010
"No, that's absolutely right"

01:05:47.010 --> 01:05:48.780
and it's interesting and it
matters to French society

01:05:48.780 --> 01:05:49.790
and so on and so forth.

01:05:49.790 --> 01:05:52.140
So the point is sharing words is tricky.

01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:53.440
But I don't even think

01:05:56.730 --> 01:06:00.573
if Joe and I, who are pretty
damn close in this pantheon,

01:06:01.440 --> 01:06:02.910
if we use a word,

01:06:02.910 --> 01:06:03.990
we may use the same word,

01:06:03.990 --> 01:06:05.760
but that doesn't mean
we mean the same thing

01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:07.260
or are getting it the same thing.

01:06:07.260 --> 01:06:09.090
And I remember saying this to John Barrois

01:06:09.090 --> 01:06:10.110
and John Perry once,

01:06:10.110 --> 01:06:12.233
and I said, "Look, I
don't think that when,

01:06:14.310 --> 01:06:15.357
you know, we use,

01:06:15.357 --> 01:06:16.860
the words mean different things

01:06:16.860 --> 01:06:18.180
in different contexts of use and stuff.

01:06:18.180 --> 01:06:19.470
And they said, "Yeah,
no, we agree with that,"

01:06:19.470 --> 01:06:22.590
but by and large, within
a sort of American society

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:24.300
in late 20th century, whatever,

01:06:24.300 --> 01:06:25.133
you know, they mean the same thing.

01:06:25.133 --> 01:06:26.670
And I said, "No, that's not right at all.

01:06:26.670 --> 01:06:28.230
I think, in fact, even
when I use the word,

01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:29.540
I'm not sure it means the same thing

01:06:29.540 --> 01:06:30.660
at the beginning of a sentence

01:06:30.660 --> 01:06:31.530
and at the end of a sentence."

01:06:31.530 --> 01:06:34.045
And they said, "Yeah, we know."

01:06:34.045 --> 01:06:38.160
So-
(Joseph laughs)

01:06:38.160 --> 01:06:38.993
I think,

01:06:43.530 --> 01:06:45.930
it's not an absolute disagreement.

01:06:45.930 --> 01:06:47.460
It's not that we,

01:06:47.460 --> 01:06:48.360
if we use the same word,

01:06:48.360 --> 01:06:49.770
that therefore we mean the same thing.

01:06:49.770 --> 01:06:51.570
I don't think it means
it's incommensurable.

01:06:51.570 --> 01:06:54.510
I don't think it means we
