01:20:29.760 --> 01:20:32.910
that anything we say is gonna
be located in this historical

01:20:32.910 --> 01:20:36.323
and grounded and contextualized
practice such that,

01:20:36.323 --> 01:20:38.370
and conceptual content for Joe

01:20:38.370 --> 01:20:41.970
is to be something meaningfully
conveyed in that practice.

01:20:41.970 --> 01:20:44.250
And so therefore, by definition,

01:20:44.250 --> 01:20:46.950
there can be no non-conceptual content."

01:20:46.950 --> 01:20:49.590
How much injustice does that do to you?

01:20:49.590 --> 01:20:53.589
- Well, I have problems with all of that.

01:20:53.589 --> 01:20:54.759
- (laughs) Okay, good.

01:20:54.759 --> 01:20:56.340
- The conceptual and the non-conceptual

01:20:56.340 --> 01:20:59.670
with the notion of content, right?

01:20:59.670 --> 01:21:01.980
That is, that I think

01:21:01.980 --> 01:21:04.450
that we can only

01:21:06.360 --> 01:21:08.670
do anything conceptual

01:21:08.670 --> 01:21:13.670
as part of a life engaged
in the world interactively

01:21:14.010 --> 01:21:15.720
in an ongoing way.

01:21:15.720 --> 01:21:20.670
And that having these practices,

01:21:20.670 --> 01:21:22.890
which are on the one hand,

01:21:22.890 --> 01:21:25.680
very, very closely interconnected.

01:21:25.680 --> 01:21:28.047
So when you think about painting practices

01:21:28.047 --> 01:21:29.910
and the ways in which they comment

01:21:29.910 --> 01:21:32.250
on previous painting practices,

01:21:32.250 --> 01:21:35.190
but also are accountable to these larger,

01:21:35.190 --> 01:21:37.920
more long distance engagements, right?

01:21:37.920 --> 01:21:41.160
I mean, impressionism
arises in the 19th century

01:21:41.160 --> 01:21:43.353
for to do with.

01:21:44.717 --> 01:21:46.500
The insufficiency,

01:21:46.500 --> 01:21:50.460
the perceived insufficiency
of a mechanistic causal

01:21:50.460 --> 01:21:51.840
in the narrow traditional sense.

01:21:51.840 --> 01:21:53.550
- Narrow traditional sense, yeah.

01:21:53.550 --> 01:21:55.801
- And so, you know,

01:21:55.801 --> 01:21:59.853
but they make sense in response to that.

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:04.440
And so, what I wanna see us talking about

01:22:04.440 --> 01:22:09.440
is the way in which to
push your point against

01:22:09.600 --> 01:22:12.643
the epistemic accounts of
the conceptual further that,

01:22:12.643 --> 01:22:16.860
I mean, I think you're conceding too

01:22:16.860 --> 01:22:21.860
to epistemic conceptions of
the accounts of conceptualness

01:22:22.020 --> 01:22:26.400
that we have to think about
concept use as practices.

01:22:26.400 --> 01:22:30.600
And referring is something people do,

01:22:30.600 --> 01:22:34.050
and they do it by
participating in practices

01:22:34.050 --> 01:22:38.970
which enable us to then make sense of

01:22:38.970 --> 01:22:43.290
and understand things like
referring beyond the light cone

01:22:43.290 --> 01:22:46.290
or pointing to those aspects of the world

01:22:46.290 --> 01:22:48.450
that we don't even have access to.

01:22:48.450 --> 01:22:49.800
- Right.
- Didn't have access.

01:22:49.800 --> 01:22:50.767
- No.
- Yeah.

01:22:50.767 --> 01:22:53.310
- In a microbial world before that.

01:22:53.310 --> 01:22:57.030
- I agree with you about
participating in these practices.

01:22:57.030 --> 01:23:00.160
I guess, a lot of people
that I interact with

01:23:01.710 --> 01:23:06.513
have an automatic causalistic
account of practice,

01:23:08.220 --> 01:23:13.220
but practices are things that
involve bumping and shoving.

01:23:13.227 --> 01:23:16.080
(Joseph laughs)

01:23:16.080 --> 01:23:18.060
You know, that are subject
to one over R-squared laws

01:23:18.060 --> 01:23:19.320
whatever, that preserve energy.

01:23:19.320 --> 01:23:22.083
I mean, all of these kinds of things.

01:23:23.670 --> 01:23:24.573
And I think,

01:23:25.860 --> 01:23:27.270
so I remember asking,

01:23:27.270 --> 01:23:30.300
did I mention this
question to Alonzo Church

01:23:30.300 --> 01:23:31.800
in the talk yesterday?

01:23:31.800 --> 01:23:32.764
Somebody...

01:23:32.764 --> 01:23:33.597
- I don't think you did.

01:23:33.597 --> 01:23:34.500
- I don't think you did.

01:23:34.500 --> 01:23:35.610
Yeah, I don't think.
- Anyway, ok.

01:23:35.610 --> 01:23:37.050
So, Alonzo Church, when
he was still alive,

01:23:37.050 --> 01:23:40.020
of course, was giving
this talk at Stanford,

01:23:40.020 --> 01:23:42.840
and I was nervous and sat on my hands

01:23:42.840 --> 01:23:43.860
and didn't think I was gonna say anything,

01:23:43.860 --> 01:23:48.860
but my imagination often
unseats my pulley tests.

01:23:48.930 --> 01:23:52.267
And I asked him after the talk,

01:23:52.267 --> 01:23:53.943
"If I refer to the sun,

01:23:55.170 --> 01:23:58.901
does it take eight minutes for
the sun to get referred to?"

01:23:58.901 --> 01:23:59.734
- No.

01:23:59.734 --> 01:24:00.927
- No.
- No, no, I know,

01:24:00.927 --> 01:24:02.340
but I just wanna tell you that.

01:24:02.340 --> 01:24:03.420
- Yeah.

01:24:03.420 --> 01:24:04.614
- There's always, they
need your quotes there.

01:24:04.614 --> 01:24:05.870
(Joseph laughs)

01:24:05.870 --> 01:24:06.703
- There from both of them.

01:24:06.703 --> 01:24:08.370
- You don't have to be
Alonzo Church to answer them.

01:24:08.370 --> 01:24:09.539
- Yes.

01:24:09.539 --> 01:24:10.372
(Joseph laughs)

01:24:10.372 --> 01:24:11.205
- But,

01:24:13.650 --> 01:24:14.483
but, two things.

01:24:14.483 --> 01:24:15.847
First of all, Alonzo Church said,

01:24:15.847 --> 01:24:18.810
"One, reference travels
at the speed of logic,"

01:24:18.810 --> 01:24:20.826
which I think was a brilliant-
(Joseph laughs)

01:24:20.826 --> 01:24:23.250
- Oh, brilliant.

01:24:23.250 --> 01:24:25.667
- Even though I have no
idea what the hell it means.

01:24:25.667 --> 01:24:27.567
(Joseph laughs)

01:24:27.567 --> 01:24:28.770
But the thing is,

01:24:28.770 --> 01:24:31.870
what matters to me is that
there is a property of the sun

01:24:33.780 --> 01:24:35.013
that it was referred to.

01:24:37.080 --> 01:24:39.360
It's not just a property of
us that we referred to it,

01:24:39.360 --> 01:24:41.280
it's actually a property of the sun,

01:24:41.280 --> 01:24:42.970
such that it was referred to

01:24:44.880 --> 01:24:47.823
and that property obtains of the sun.

01:24:48.810 --> 01:24:50.910
And now the relativity gets tricky.

01:24:50.910 --> 01:24:53.563
Does it obtain of the sun right now?

01:24:53.563 --> 01:24:55.470
I mean, is that even a coherent,

01:24:55.470 --> 01:24:58.024
I have to defer it, Priya, here.

01:24:58.024 --> 01:24:58.857
- Well, I dunno,

01:24:58.857 --> 01:25:01.650
I think we could get into
a big controversy now.

01:25:01.650 --> 01:25:02.483
I think I was,

01:25:02.483 --> 01:25:04.410
I will escorted avoid this about

01:25:04.410 --> 01:25:07.353
whether it's really a property of the sun.

01:25:07.353 --> 01:25:08.520
I mean, I think the eight minutes

01:25:08.520 --> 01:25:10.735
is not a property of the sun.

01:25:10.735 --> 01:25:11.568
It's a-
- Well, no,

01:25:11.568 --> 01:25:13.260
but it's got to be a property of the sun

01:25:13.260 --> 01:25:16.563
in the sense that if I
think a thought about you,

01:25:17.820 --> 01:25:19.440
that's about you,

01:25:19.440 --> 01:25:21.911
that's gotta be a property of you

01:25:21.911 --> 01:25:23.100
that you were thought about.

01:25:23.100 --> 01:25:24.450
I think if you lose that,

01:25:24.450 --> 01:25:25.283
you lose,

01:25:26.130 --> 01:25:29.342
you know, we're in the
Trump Right-Wing Vortex.

01:25:29.342 --> 01:25:31.225
(Joseph laughs)
- I don't quite think so.

01:25:31.225 --> 01:25:32.610
I don't quite think so.

01:25:32.610 --> 01:25:33.443
But let me,

01:25:33.443 --> 01:25:36.600
I think Peter Morgan has
been very patiently waiting

01:25:36.600 --> 01:25:38.460
with his question.

01:25:38.460 --> 01:25:40.860
And, well, he has a couple of questions.

01:25:40.860 --> 01:25:43.777
So he quotes, said by
Professor Rouse, in paraphrase,

01:25:43.777 --> 01:25:47.280
"There is more in the world
than we can conceptualize.

01:25:47.280 --> 01:25:48.810
There is that in the world

01:25:48.810 --> 01:25:51.270
that we have so far conceptualized."

01:25:51.270 --> 01:25:52.103
- Yeah.

01:25:52.103 --> 01:25:54.780
- "How do we fix or place what we discover

01:25:54.780 --> 01:25:56.670
and conceptualize tomorrow

01:25:56.670 --> 01:25:59.670
into what we have so far conceptualized?

01:25:59.670 --> 01:26:01.950
Are there ideas of distance

01:26:01.950 --> 01:26:04.950
and betweenness as systematic indications

01:26:04.950 --> 01:26:07.050
of how surprised we are by new discoveries

01:26:07.050 --> 01:26:09.030
and conceptualizations?

01:26:09.030 --> 01:26:11.670
And this also plays to what
Professor Rouse just said,

01:26:11.670 --> 01:26:15.000
an ongoing discussion places
new utterances relative

01:26:15.000 --> 01:26:16.650
to previous utterances,

01:26:16.650 --> 01:26:18.390
which then can be adjudicated

01:26:18.390 --> 01:26:20.460
and worked out systematically.

01:26:20.460 --> 01:26:22.373
We have to allow a story
to continue, right?"

01:26:22.373 --> 01:26:24.173
I mean, that's his sort of question.

01:26:25.014 --> 01:26:25.847
- And, of course,

01:26:25.847 --> 01:26:28.440
you know, the notion of distance here

01:26:28.440 --> 01:26:30.930
is not spatial distance, right?

01:26:30.930 --> 01:26:35.460
I mean, microbes that
were inconceivable to us,

01:26:35.460 --> 01:26:37.290
except, you know,

01:26:37.290 --> 01:26:38.400
in the sense of,

01:26:38.400 --> 01:26:40.983
well, there might be such
things that we can't,

01:26:42.540 --> 01:26:44.250
you know-
- An abstract way, right?

01:26:44.250 --> 01:26:45.083
- Yeah, that's right.

01:26:45.083 --> 01:26:47.670
In an abstract and empty way.

01:26:47.670 --> 01:26:49.590
But that notion becomes concrete

01:26:49.590 --> 01:26:54.590
and rich once you are
able to engage with that.

01:26:55.020 --> 01:26:58.980
And, of course, you can
only do that by drawing on

01:26:58.980 --> 01:27:01.350
and extending the resources,

01:27:01.350 --> 01:27:06.350
including the material
practices that you already have.

01:27:06.540 --> 01:27:08.850
I mean, this is one of the key features

01:27:08.850 --> 01:27:11.880
that now thinking about
philosophy of scientific practice

01:27:11.880 --> 01:27:12.713
that is,

01:27:12.713 --> 01:27:15.150
you know, that the sciences exemplified,

01:27:15.150 --> 01:27:19.590
taking what the relationships they uncover

01:27:19.590 --> 01:27:22.410
and now turning those into resources

01:27:22.410 --> 01:27:25.410
for bringing out other things.

01:27:25.410 --> 01:27:28.330
That's part of how the sciences expand

01:27:30.180 --> 01:27:32.010
you know, the realm of the conceptual.

01:27:32.010 --> 01:27:33.540
And also, of course,

01:27:33.540 --> 01:27:35.970
close off aspects,
things that it once seem

01:27:35.970 --> 01:27:38.190
to make sense to talk about

01:27:38.190 --> 01:27:41.130
and now only makes sense to talk about

01:27:41.130 --> 01:27:43.590
in quotation marks, right?

01:27:43.590 --> 01:27:44.423
You know, I-

01:27:44.423 --> 01:27:45.720
- Right, I mean, and I think-

01:27:45.720 --> 01:27:48.690
- Talking about when they
referred to phlogiston.

01:27:48.690 --> 01:27:49.620
- Yeah, right.

01:27:49.620 --> 01:27:51.540
- We no longer allow that.
- But, I think, right,

01:27:51.540 --> 01:27:53.850
this gets at the heart of the fact

01:27:53.850 --> 01:27:58.850
that scientific discovery
as we refer to it,

01:27:59.250 --> 01:28:00.990
is inherently provisional.

01:28:00.990 --> 01:28:03.210
I mean, that science itself is really,

01:28:03.210 --> 01:28:06.120
our conceptual understanding
at any given time

01:28:06.120 --> 01:28:08.070
is deeply provisional and that-

01:28:08.070 --> 01:28:11.070
- It's also temporarily
extended in directions.

01:28:11.070 --> 01:28:16.070
It's drawing on a history that
gives us open-ended resources

01:28:16.650 --> 01:28:20.040
and allows it to be to
point beyond itself.

01:28:20.040 --> 01:28:21.297
- Yeah.

01:28:21.297 --> 01:28:23.640
- And in ways that,

01:28:23.640 --> 01:28:25.350
and, you know, you talked earlier

01:28:25.350 --> 01:28:27.963
about not being able to define concepts.

01:28:28.800 --> 01:28:33.800
That's because the concepts
always involve more than

01:28:33.840 --> 01:28:38.073
we're able to articulate
now in detail, right?

01:28:39.330 --> 01:28:44.330
Typically, we have an opening
of a space of exploration

01:28:46.710 --> 01:28:48.660
and the words we use.

01:28:48.660 --> 01:28:51.393
I mean, think of the
history of the word gene.

01:28:52.228 --> 01:28:53.061
- Oh, yeah.

01:28:53.061 --> 01:28:58.037
- And the way it went from
a merely hypothetical entity

01:28:58.980 --> 01:29:01.950
to physical,

01:29:01.950 --> 01:29:05.190
to chemical, to structural,

01:29:05.190 --> 01:29:08.820
to information theoretical in quotes,

01:29:08.820 --> 01:29:12.903
to then being these complicated
regulatory networks.

01:29:14.190 --> 01:29:15.023
- Right, then-

01:29:15.023 --> 01:29:17.530
- Turned out to be also epigenetically.

01:29:17.530 --> 01:29:20.970
- Yeah, it's become an
amalgam of everything

01:29:20.970 --> 01:29:22.470
that it meant, right?

01:29:22.470 --> 01:29:24.930
- Yeah.
- So, sort of...

01:29:24.930 --> 01:29:25.763
So there-
- And you have

01:29:25.763 --> 01:29:26.596
to think about it,

01:29:26.596 --> 01:29:31.170
and of course, how the use of that word

01:29:31.170 --> 01:29:32.697
is gonna get tied into practices

01:29:32.697 --> 01:29:34.380
and what follows from it,

01:29:34.380 --> 01:29:35.460
including, of course,

01:29:35.460 --> 01:29:38.430
all the other non-scientific resonances.

01:29:38.430 --> 01:29:42.720
The way in which is history
was connected to notion,

01:29:42.720 --> 01:29:45.900
and especially the information notion of,

01:29:45.900 --> 01:29:48.180
notions of an immaterial soul

01:29:48.180 --> 01:29:50.610
that is at the heart of the body.

01:29:50.610 --> 01:29:53.250
That that inferential
connection was so crucial

01:29:53.250 --> 01:29:55.323
to the history of 20th century biology.

01:29:57.150 --> 01:29:59.700
- There was a great
thing that John Hoagland

01:29:59.700 --> 01:30:01.446
and I used to say that,

01:30:01.446 --> 01:30:04.530
of course, there have
always been electrons,

01:30:04.530 --> 01:30:08.367
but there have only always been
electrons since Rutherford.

01:30:08.367 --> 01:30:09.780
(Joseph chuckles)

01:30:09.780 --> 01:30:14.010
- Right, oh, so let's not
much as I am fascinated

01:30:14.010 --> 01:30:15.120
by Bruno Latour,

01:30:15.120 --> 01:30:19.023
let's not go down the
Latourian tunnel here with,

01:30:20.130 --> 01:30:22.770
whether a pastour (laughs) whether...

01:30:22.770 --> 01:30:24.400
- All right.

01:30:24.400 --> 01:30:25.607
- Right, and the all-

01:30:25.607 --> 01:30:27.810
- You know, Bruno tried to get
at some of that in one way,

01:30:27.810 --> 01:30:30.330
but that's not quite
what Brian was saying.

01:30:30.330 --> 01:30:31.350
- Right.

01:30:31.350 --> 01:30:32.489
No, no, yeah, yeah.

01:30:32.489 --> 01:30:33.397
But I think it-

01:30:33.397 --> 01:30:36.300
- And it is closely connected
to this temporal externalism

01:30:36.300 --> 01:30:38.280
that I'm emphasizing as well.

01:30:38.280 --> 01:30:39.212
I mean....

01:30:39.212 --> 01:30:40.260
- Right.
- You and John

01:30:40.260 --> 01:30:41.790
and I actually have a lot of,

01:30:41.790 --> 01:30:43.593
a lot in common on that and-

01:30:44.850 --> 01:30:45.840
- Right, we do, we do.

01:30:45.840 --> 01:30:50.840
That's why these disagreements
are so unnerving.

01:30:51.125 --> 01:30:52.500
(Priya chuckles)
(Brian chuckles)

01:30:52.500 --> 01:30:53.340
- Well, it's be,

01:30:53.340 --> 01:30:55.500
you know, I mean, this was a lovely point

01:30:55.500 --> 01:30:57.360
that Ian Hacking once made.

01:30:57.360 --> 01:31:00.030
If two people disagree point by point

01:31:00.030 --> 01:31:03.540
on a half a dozen issues
that they also take

01:31:03.540 --> 01:31:05.070
to be really centrally important,

01:31:05.070 --> 01:31:07.688
it means they agree on
almost everything else.

01:31:07.688 --> 01:31:08.521
- Right, right.

01:31:08.521 --> 01:31:12.630
- That very nicely characterizes
you and me. (chuckles)

01:31:12.630 --> 01:31:15.930
- So I think before we close,

01:31:15.930 --> 01:31:19.020
there's a request from
one of our guests, Steven,

01:31:19.020 --> 01:31:19.853
who came up.

01:31:19.853 --> 01:31:24.079
Would Professor Rouse be
willing to share his slides?

01:31:24.079 --> 01:31:25.613
- Yes, happy to.
- And willing to try before,

01:31:25.613 --> 01:31:30.613
yeah, and we can then add
that onto our video or-

01:31:30.630 --> 01:31:33.332
- Yes, so should I send
that to Ty or to you?

01:31:33.332 --> 01:31:36.360
- Yeah, could you please
go ahead to send it to Ty-

01:31:36.360 --> 01:31:37.800
- I will do that.

01:31:37.800 --> 01:31:38.640
- Thank you, thank you.

01:31:38.640 --> 01:31:39.930
I'd like those, too.

01:31:39.930 --> 01:31:41.550
- Yeah, so I think,

01:31:41.550 --> 01:31:45.990
let me see if there is a quick question,

01:31:45.990 --> 01:31:47.510
so that I...

01:31:48.997 --> 01:31:50.970
I think, okay, there's a question,

01:31:50.970 --> 01:31:51.803
it's not gonna be quick,

01:31:51.803 --> 01:31:55.327
but we'll try to wrap it up soon.

01:31:55.327 --> 01:31:57.420
"So listening to this
wonderful conversation,"

01:31:57.420 --> 01:31:58.807
it's June Lao again,

01:31:58.807 --> 01:32:01.740
"Wonders whether Brian and
Joe understand subjectivity,

01:32:01.740 --> 01:32:03.540
scientific practices, human language,

01:32:03.540 --> 01:32:05.130
et cetera, differently.

01:32:05.130 --> 01:32:07.200
It seems that for Brian's subjectivity

01:32:07.200 --> 01:32:10.200
in any sort of human doings
is as non-conceptual,

01:32:10.200 --> 01:32:12.300
as the world is non-conceptual.

01:32:12.300 --> 01:32:13.830
Similar to a digital circuit,

01:32:13.830 --> 01:32:15.510
even with all the correct arrangement,

01:32:15.510 --> 01:32:17.850
it still needs to be taken to be digital

01:32:17.850 --> 01:32:19.350
for it to be digital.

01:32:19.350 --> 01:32:21.540
But for Joe, this does not seem necessary.

01:32:21.540 --> 01:32:23.307
Is this a fair understanding?"

01:32:30.990 --> 01:32:32.190
- I agree with John,

01:32:32.190 --> 01:32:33.963
so it's up to Joe.

01:32:35.520 --> 01:32:39.512
- Well, subjectivity is
typically not one of my words.

01:32:39.512 --> 01:32:40.923
(chuckles) Right?

01:32:42.000 --> 01:32:45.750
I think it has too many
problematic connotations

01:32:45.750 --> 01:32:47.880
which don't belong together.

01:32:47.880 --> 01:32:52.290
Many of the things that people
talk about in those terms

01:32:52.290 --> 01:32:55.200
are really important, okay?

01:32:55.200 --> 01:32:56.373
And, you know,

01:32:58.440 --> 01:33:02.820
the kinds of discovery of subjectivity,

01:33:02.820 --> 01:33:06.510
whether it's in things like
impressionism in painting

01:33:06.510 --> 01:33:11.510
or in the Interior Monologues of Joyce

01:33:11.550 --> 01:33:13.980
and so forth, right?

01:33:13.980 --> 01:33:17.700
Is itself a reconception,

01:33:17.700 --> 01:33:18.533
is, you know,

01:33:18.533 --> 01:33:22.320
in this sense, we now wanna say,

01:33:22.320 --> 01:33:24.240
that before Joyce

01:33:24.240 --> 01:33:27.003
there were interior
monologues (chuckles) right?

01:33:27.960 --> 01:33:32.960
You didn't have the same
conception of interiority, right?

01:33:35.068 --> 01:33:36.750
For it to show up that way.

01:33:36.750 --> 01:33:41.750
So the kinds of things
people wanna talk about

01:33:42.240 --> 01:33:44.790
in that way are important,

01:33:44.790 --> 01:33:47.880
but we needed a better vocabulary,

01:33:47.880 --> 01:33:51.000
and a better set of practices
for engaging with it.

01:33:51.000 --> 01:33:54.390
Many of which have this kind
of character of subjectivity

01:33:54.390 --> 01:33:57.090
as a kind of self-enclosure, right?

01:33:57.090 --> 01:33:59.073
Which, I think, you know,

01:33:59.910 --> 01:34:04.080
the subjective inner and the
causal interactive outer,

01:34:04.080 --> 01:34:08.644
and that just seems to me
really distorting of all.

01:34:08.644 --> 01:34:09.960
- Right.

01:34:09.960 --> 01:34:11.310
- So that would be my answer.

01:34:11.310 --> 01:34:13.380
- Okay, great.

01:34:13.380 --> 01:34:15.030
Although we could keep going,

01:34:15.030 --> 01:34:19.410
I think, in the interest
of our audience's stamina,

01:34:19.410 --> 01:34:22.470
we should probably bring this to a close.

01:34:22.470 --> 01:34:26.760
And I just wanted to say
thank you very much to Brian

01:34:26.760 --> 01:34:29.760
